{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Identifier versus Predictive Attribute.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Identifier versus Predictive Attribute.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"identifier versus predictive attribute identifier versus predictive attribute","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Identifier versus Predictive Attribute.md","fileName":"Identifier versus Predictive Attribute.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Identifier versus Predictive Attribute","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 09:05\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Data Science Framing and Representation]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Data Science Framing and Representation","datascience_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Identifier versus Predictive Attribute.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Identifier versus Predictive Attribute.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"identifier versus predictive attribute identifier versus predictive attribute","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Identifier versus Predictive Attribute.md","fileName":"Identifier versus Predictive Attribute.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Identifier versus Predictive Attribute","heading":"Identifier versus Predictive Attribute","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An identifier labels an instance so its records can be joined, checked, or traced. Although it may occupy a column in an analytic table, the identifier is generally not a substantive predictor of the target. Treating arbitrary ID values as meaningful features can invite accidental patterns.\n\nThe distinction belongs to data understanding: a column's machine-readable type does not reveal its analytical role. A number can be an ID, a count, an ordered rating, or a measured quantity, and those roles permit different transformations and interpretati